A perfect number equals the sum of its proper divisors, that is, every positive divisor except the number itself, and any n below 1 must give False.

The submission returns False for n < 1 and for n == 1, which is right for 1 because it has no proper divisors and the empty sum is 0. The rest is a trial-division loop:

    divisors = {1}
    i = 2
    while i * i <= n:
        if n % i == 0:
            divisors.add(i)
            other = n // i
            if other != n:
                divisors.add(other)

after which it returns whether the sum of the set equals n. Each divisor i found up to the square root is added together with its partner n // i, so every divisor below n ends up in the set. Because i starts at 2, the partner is at most n / 2 and n itself is never added; the guard on other never actually triggers. Collecting into a set matters for perfect squares: for n = 16 the loop reaches i = 4, whose partner is also 4, and the set stores it only once.

I traced n = 496, which none of the visible asserts use. The loop runs i up to 22 and picks up the pairs 2 and 248, 4 and 124, 8 and 62, 16 and 31. With the initial 1, those sum to 496, so the function returns True, which is correct. For n = 12 the set becomes 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6, summing to 16, and the function correctly returns False. Nothing in the code refers to 6, 28 or 10; the same loop and comparison apply to any positive integer.

